      "title": "How it works",
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## OneShot MV: app\n\nThis workflow opens as an **app**: the settings on the right, the results in the middle. **App / Graph** at the top left switches to this node graph and back. It is the main workflow with the main settings shown and friendlier names (**Preview only** is **preview_only**).\n\n**A song and a World in, a music video out.** Load a song, write a World, click **Run**.\n\n**1. Preview first**\nTurn **preview_only** on and click **Run**. The Director:\n- analyses the song,\n- writes the World,\n- plans every shot.\n\nThe summary lists every shot: time, energy, who, wearing what, and where. Nothing renders yet. Change the World line if you need to, turn **preview_only** off, and click **Run** again. The song analysis and the World are cached, so the second run starts faster.\n\n**2. The World**\nOne line is enough: *the gladiator games in ancient Rome*. It decides who the characters are, what they wear, the places, the light and the grade.\n\nFor more control, write it the way the Studio's examples are written: the tone, then the people in the order you want them, then the places, then the light. What you say about the cast is a rule (\"no men\", \"present day\", \"no people, all robots\"), and a character you describe keeps your words.\n\nThe characters you name are cast first, in that order (**cast_order** `as written`; `mixed` shuffles them). Every shot is a different person.\n\n**Who sings** (**cast**): `crowd` casts a different invented person for each line. `single artist` has one person sing every line: un-bypass **Artist photo** and pick a photo of yourself or of someone who agreed to it, or leave it bypassed and describe a new person in **artist_description** (the tool makes their headshot first). The crowd behind the singer stays off unless **crowd** is `on`. It needs FLUX.2 Klein, the LTX union-control LoRA and comfyui_controlnet_aux (see the README).\n\n**The edit**\nThe song is mapped into three energy levels, each cut its own way (**cuts_low / cuts_medium / cuts_high**):\n- **low** (the intro, quiet bridges): a cut every 2 bars.\n- **medium** (verses, builds): a cut every bar.\n- **high** (choruses, the drop): cuts to the music, each to a new face in the same spot.\n\n**audio_fx**: `subtle` (the default) adds flash, zoom, glow and colour split on the drums. `intense` is stronger and adds a motion-blurred twitch. `none` turns them off.\n\n**crowd**: `auto` puts a soft-focus crowd behind the singers when the World is a packed spectacle (an arena, a stadium, crowded stands); otherwise nobody else is in frame.\n\n**Take QC** checks every take and re-renders bad ones, at most doubling the render time. With the SyncNet weights installed (see the README), it also scores lip sync. A vision check looks at every picture before any video is made: one person, the right gender, no stray glasses or writing, period-right clothes.\n\n**broll_share**: about 1 shot in 8 becomes a cutaway where nobody sings, never on the drop. 0 turns it off.\n\n**quality**: `quality` is LTX-2.3 in 3 stages at 50 fps; `fast` uses the same model at 25 fps with fewer steps. **models**: `auto` picks the full set on a 48 GB+ card when its files are installed, else the 24 GB set.\n\n**seconds**: 0 renders the whole song. **start**: -1 picks the strongest stretch.\n\nThe finished MP4 lands in `output/oneshot-mv/runs/<run>/final_<format>.mp4`.\n\nUse music you have the rights to."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
